  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1116 AM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nABUNDANT MOISTURE THROUGH FRIDAY:\n\nLow pressure located to our west continues to keep us under \nsouthwest flow aloft. This flow has ushered in highly anamolous \nmoisture, which has resulted in a large rain shield that covers \nalmost the entire area. As the day progresses this rain will move \noff to the northeast and or scatter out. As of now the rainfall \nrates are steady and not really exceeding 0.50\". The big question \ntoday is how much clearing will happen and where. Additional shower \nand storm development is expected though out the day, but will any \nof these storms be strong and able to produce heavy rainfall. Places \nthat get sunshine this afternoon have the better chances of more \nrobust storms. Given all the rain the past several days soils are \ngetting saturated with not much break. This increases the potential \nfor flash flooding and debris flows. Models continue to show \nthat the southern San Juans could get up to an additional inch \nof rain by tonight. Showers and storms are expected to linger \ninto the evening with a gradual decrease in coverage overnight.\n\nThe pocket of record setting moisture will move off to our east \ntonight, but the plume stays in place due to the low out west. \nTomorrow that low tracks over the northern Great Basin. It is \nuncertain how much forcing we will get if any, but the associated \njet is expected to spread overhead. This could provide some forcing \nas well as shear. Given the moisture another round of showers and \nstorms is expected mainly in the afternoon and evening. Like today \nplaces that get more sunshine have the better chances for stronger \nstorms. With the shear in place a few severe storms are possible \nalong with that the potential for heavy rainfall. Showers and storms \nlook to linger Friday night as the low pressure moves closer to the \narea. \n\nMOISTURE DECREASES:\n\nOn Saturday the low pressure passes to our north, so expect chances \nfor showers and storms once again. The mositure values are supposed \nto decrease, which may only result in a less coverage of convection. \nIt uncertain if there will still be a chance for heavy rain. On \nSunday the low lifts out over the Plains causing drier air to filter \nin from the west. Also, subsidence may put a cap on any convection. \nAs a result most places will be dry although lingering moisture \nacross north central CO could support a few showers there. Is it \npossible the models are trying to push the moisture out to quickly \nso could not rule our isolated showers mainly over the higher \nterrain. Monday looks to be quiet as well. Models are indicating \nanother low pressure trough moving into the Great Basin and \nincreasing storm coverage once again mid next week.",
